  "summary": "Monolithic stone-jumping, dragon-topped houses and carved ancestor columns of warrior Nias.",
  "description": "On Nias, boys proved manhood by hompa batu — leaping a two-metre basalt wall — while villages raised megalithic tribute stones, carved ancestor seats (daru-daru) and dragon-beamed chief's houses (omah) whose giant central hearths and ship-shaped roofs still stand. Gold-crowned warrior regalia, flexed-pose ancestor figures and pig feasts marked rank in a famously martial society. German missionary rule ended war and stone leaping survived as heritage spectacle; UNESCO-flagged architecture and stone sculpture anchor the island's identity.",
